 blocked, so claiming a corner like (0,0) threatens multiple lines and opens paths to win or draw while limiting X's options</reason>\n<answer>O(0,0)</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player X.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 O . .\n1 . X .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nX(0,1), X(0,2), X(1,0), X(1,2), X(2,0), X(2,1), X(2,2)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Blocking O's potential win and creating a winning opportunity by threatening a diagonal or row with X's presence</reason>\n<answer>X(0,1)</answer>", "episode_score": 1.62, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Blocking O's potential win and creating a winning opportunity by threatening a diagonal or row with X's presence</reason>\n<answer>X(0,1)</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player O.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 O X .\n1 . X .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nO(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Center and corner control are key; O already has a corner (0,0), and placing at (2,2) threatens a diagonal win and expands control over the board</reason>\n<answer>O(2,2)</answer>", "episode_score": -1.8, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Center and corner control are key; O already has a corner (0,0), and placing at (2,2) threatens a diagonal win and expands control over the board</reason>\n<answer>O(2,2)</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player X.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 O X .\n1 . X .\n2 . . O\n\nLegal moves:\nX(0,2), X(1,0), X(1,2), X(2,0), X(2,1)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Blocking O's potential win on row 1 and creating a winning opportunity on column 2 or diagonal by placing at (0,2) to complete a vertical win with existing X at (1,1) and (2,2) is not valid; instead, (2,1) completes a potential diagonal with (1,1) and (0,0), but (0,0) is empty. The key is (1,0) to threaten a column and block O's center control—(1,0) opens a path to win vertically and blocks O's potential win at (1,1)-(1,2). However, (2,1) is critical to stop O from winning in center column. But (1,2) is open and allows X to complete a horizontal win with (1,1) and (1,0). Actually, (0,2) is best: if X plays (0,2), it forms a vertical with (1,1) and (2,2)? No. Wait: (0,2) is empty. Current board: row0: O X .; row1: . X .; row2: . . O. So (0,2) is empty. (1,0) and (1,2) are empty. (2,0) and (2,1) are empty. O has (0,0), (1,1), (2,2). So O has a diagonal. If X plays (2,1), O cannot win on diagonal. But if X plays (1,0), X threatens (0,0) and (1,1), but (0,0) is O. So (1,0) doesn't help. (2,1) blocks O's diagonal win from (0,0) to (2,2). That is the only threat. So (2,1) stops O from winning. Also, (2,1) allows X to later win with (2,0), (2,2), or (1,1). But (2,1) directly blocks the only diagonal win. So best move is (2,1).</reason>\n<answer>X(2,1)</answer>", "episode_score": 0.967, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Blocking O's potential win on row 1 and creating a winning opportunity on column 2 or diagonal by placing at (0,2) to complete a vertical win with existing X at (1,1) and (2,2) is not valid; instead, (2,1) completes a potential diagonal with (1,1) and (0,0), but (0,0) is empty. The key is (1,0) to threaten a column and block O's center control—(1,0) opens a path to win vertically and blocks O's potential win at (1,1)-(1,2). However, (2,1) is critical to stop O from winning in center column. But (1,2) is open and allows X to complete a horizontal win with (1,1) and (1,0). Actually, (0,2) is best: if X plays (0,2), it forms a vertical with (1,1) and (2,2)? No. Wait: (0,2) is empty. Current board: row0: O X .; row1: . X .; row2: . . O. So (0,2) is empty. (1,0) and (1,2) are empty. (2,0) and (2,1) are empty. O has (0,0), (1,1), (2,2). So O has a diagonal. If X plays (2,1), O cannot win on diagonal. But if X plays (1,0), X threatens (0,0) and (1,1), but (0,0) is O. So (1,0) doesn't help. (2,1) blocks O's diagonal win from (0,0) to (2,2). That is the only threat. So (2,1) stops O from winning. Also, (2,1) allows X to later win with (2,0), (2,2), or (1,1). But (2,1) directly blocks the only diagonal win. So best move is (2,1).</reason>\n<answer>X(2,1)</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player X.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 . . .\n1 . . .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nX(0,0), X(0,1), X(0,2), X(1,0), X(1,1), X(1,2), X(2,0), X(2,1), X(2,2)assistant\n", "