tical path; blocking O from completing a line is key. Central control and defense against X's potential win are vital. O(1,0) threatens a vertical win with O(0,0) and O(2,0), but also aligns with X at (1,2) and (2,1), which could lead to a diagonal. However, the most immediate threat to X’s potential win is at (1,1), which is empty. But since that's not a legal move, we consider best defense. Instead, O(1,1) is not legal. The board has no X in middle. The most effective move is to claim center (1,1) to control the middle, but it's not listed. So among available moves, O(0,1) would threaten a vertical with (1,1) and (2,1), but X is at (1,2). Best move is to threaten center or block X's path. But X has only one piece. X is at (1,2). X can win by (0,1)-(1,2)-(2,0)? No. (0,2)-(1,2)-(2,2)? No. (0,0)-(1,1)-(2,2)? Not active. So only real threat is (1,1). So the best move to prevent X from winning is to take (1,1), but not available. So among legal moves, the only one that helps create a strong position is (0,1), which enables future center and vertical expansion. But actually, the best move is to block possible future win. X can win if it gets (1,1), (2,2), or (0,2), etc. The only immediate win path for X is not clear. Best to expand in center. Since (1,1) is missing, and O has no pieces, best is to play in center — not available. So only move that gives control is (1,0) — connects to (0,0) and (2,0). But (0,1) or (1,1) would be better. But (1,1) not allowed. So among available moves, (1,0) is not central. Wait — all moves are legal. But (1,1) is missing. So only center is missing. So best is to go to (1,1) — not possible. So the best legal move that maximizes chance is to go to (0,1), to start forming vertical and diagonal, but actually, best is to go to (1,1) — not available. So among options, (2,1) or (0,1) or (1,0) — (1,0) is adjacent to X at"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\