ith (0,0), (1,1), (2,2)? That's a diagonal.\n\nSo if O does not play at (2,2), X can complete diagonal.\n\nTherefore, to prevent X from winning, O must play at (2,2).\n\nThus, O plays (2,2) to block X's diagonal win.\n\nThis move also creates a potential for future wins — if X plays elsewhere, O can respond.\n\nSo best move is to block X’s only possible win.\n\n<answer>O(2,2)</answer>", "episode_score": -1.097, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Player O can win by placing at (1,0), completing a diagonal from (0,1) to (1,0) to (2,2) after opponent's move at (1,1), but since opponent already has (1,1) blocked, instead, (1,0) opens a potential winning line with center (1,1) and (2,2) — but actually, better: (2,2) completes the vertical column with (0,2) and (1,2) — currently (0,2) and (1,2) are empty. Actually, current board: (0,1)=O, (1,1)=X, so center is taken. Instead, O can now play at (2,0) to threaten center and form a column. But best: O at (2,2) threatens diagonal from (0,1) to (1,2) to (2,3)? No. Let's check lines: O at (0,1), X at (1,1). O at (2,2) would complete no line. But (2,0) connects with (1,0) and (0,0)? (0,0) is empty. Best winning opportunity: (1,2) — O already at (0,1), X at (1,1). O at (1,2) would complete the top-right diagonal with (0,1), (1,2), (2,3)? No. Only (0,1) to (1,2) to (2,3) — invalid.\n\nWait: check if any line is already formed — O at (0,1), X at (1,1), others empty. O cannot win immediately. So best to block X from winning and threaten.\n\nX has (0,0)? No. X at (0,0)? No — X at (0,0)? No — given: row0: X O . → (0,0)=X, (0,1)=O, (0,2)=.\n\nRow1: . X . → (1,0)=., (1,1)=X, (1,2)=.\n\nRow2: all .\n\nSo X has (0,0), (1,1). Could X win with (0,0), (1,1), (2,2)? That's a diagonal.\n\nSo if O does not play at (2,2), X can complete diagonal.\n\nTherefore, to prevent X from winning, O must play at (2,2).\n\nThus, O plays (2,2) to block X's diagonal win.\n\nThis move also creates a potential for future wins — if X plays elsewhere, O can respond.\n\nSo 