 to threaten a win. (2,0) connects to nothing. (0,1) is center-left — no immediate win. But look: (1,1) is X, so (0,1) is not a winning move. What about (2,1)? That would not complete anything. Actually, no immediate win possible. But O must block or threaten. X at (1,1) — if O plays (0,1), that’s not winning. The only winning line is if O completes a line. Currently, no row, column, or diagonal has two Os. So no win possible. Therefore, best is to prevent X from winning. X has only one piece. So all moves are neutral — but O wants to maximize win > draw > loss. With only one X, O can win in two moves. So best first move is to center or edge. But center is taken. (1,1) is taken. So O should play at (0,1) to threaten center column — but X is already at (1,1). Still, O can play at (2,1) to mirror. Actually, no threat. But only one move. Without a clear win, best is to open a path. But O must maximize win. Since no immediate win, and X is isolated, O can force a draw. But which move leads to better position? Playing at (0,0) opens row 0. (2,2) opens diagonal. But the center is occupied. However, (0,0) and (2,2) are on diagonal with (1,1). So O at (0,0) threatens diagonal. (2,2) same. But X is at (1,1), so if O plays (0,0), that doesn't complete."}]
