[2026-08-06 10:14:19] [agentic_pipeline.py (299)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 987875] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.Information of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\nX(0,0), X(0,1), X(0,2), X(1,0), X(1,1), X(1,2), X(2,0), X(2,1), X(2,2).\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\nX(1,1)\n\n\nYou are player O.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 . . .\n1 . X .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nO(0,0), O(0,1), O(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Player O can create a winning opportunity by blocking the opponent’s potential win and establishing control; the middle of the board is key in forming winning lines, and the center column (1,0) connects with the open space at (0,0), (2,0), and (1,2) — but most importantly, by placing at (0,0), O can start a diagonal from (0,0) to (1,1) to (2,2), though that’s not filled yet. However, the only immediate win possibility is not directly visible. Instead, observe that the opponent has only one X at (1,1), so no immediate threat — yet, O must occupy a central or strategic position. The most effective and immediate move is to control symmetry — but since no immediate win exists, blocking or initiating dominance is essential. The most impactful move is (1,0), as it controls the middle-left vertical line and enables a potential row, column, or diagonal extension in the future — and (1,0) directly threatens to complete a vertical line (1,0)-(1,1)-(1,2), which is open — no, opponent only has (1,1). So, (1,0) is a strong move to expand. Actually, observe: no one has a 3-in-a-row, so O can only win by creating one. Since the opponent has only one X, and O can mirror or extend, best first move is to secure a middle column. But the board is empty — the only winning opportunity is if O creates a row/column/diagonal with two O's and one more. So, the move that best sets up for a win or draw is (0,1), because it's not adjacent to opponent, and it's central — no, actually (1,1) is occupied. So, the center is blocked. Therefore, the only logical path is to go into a position where future expansion is possible — all are equal, so the best is to take a middle of a line. But O must avoid being trapped. The safest is to go (0,1)? But again, all positions are open. But the only way to win is to create a line. The most promising move is to place in the center — but center is taken. So, the best is (2,2)? But that doesn't connect. Actually, the only possible win for O is if it creates a full row or column. The best choice is to go in (1,2), because (1,1) is already occupied, so O cannot form a full row in row 1 — only two positions. So, row 1 has X at (1,1), so if O places at (1,0), then row 1 will be X-O-?, not a win. Column 0: empty — if O places at (0,0),", "episode_score": 0.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<reason>Player O can create a winning opportunity by blocking the opponent’s potential win and establishing control; the middle of the board is key in forming winning lines, and the center column (1,0) connects with the open space at (0,0), (2,0), and (1,2) — but most importantly, by placing at (0,0), O can start a diagonal from (0,0) to (1,1) to (2,2), though that’s not filled yet. However, the only immediate win possibility is not directly visible. Instead, observe that the opponent has only one X at (1,1), so no immediate threat — yet, O must occupy a central or strategic position. The most effective and immediate move is to control symmetry — but since no immediate win exists, blocking or initiating dominance is essential. The most impactful move is (1,0), as it controls the middle-left vertical line and enables a potential row, column, or diagonal extension in the future — and (1,0) directly threatens to complete a vertical line (1,0)-(1,1)-(1,2), which is open — no, opponent only has (1,1). So, (1,0) is a strong move to expand. Actually, observe: no one has a 3-in-a-row, so O can only win by creating one. Since the opponent has only one X, and O can mirror or extend, best first move is to secure a middle column. But the board is empty — the only winning opportunity is if O creates a row/column/diagonal with two O's and one more. So, the move that best sets up for a win or draw is (0,1), because it's not adjacent to opponent, and it's central — no, actually (1,1) is occupied. So, the center is blocked. Therefore, the only logical path is to go into a position where future expansion is possible — all are equal, so the best is to take a middle of a line. But O must avoid being trapped. The safest is to go (0,1)? But again, all positions are open. But the only way to win is to create a line. The most promising move is to place in the center — but center is taken. So, the best is (2,2)? But that doesn't connect. Actually, the only possible win for O is if it creates a full row or column. The best choice is to go in (1,2), because (1,1) is already occupied, so O cannot form a full row in row 1 — only two positions. So, row 1 has X at (1,1), so if O places at (1,0), then row 1 will be X-O-?, not a win. Column 0: empty — if O places at (0,0),<|im_end|>", "tag": "TicTacToe-second-100", "minimax_decision_records": [{"turn_index": 0, "player_id": 1, "action": "", "valid": 0.0, "format_valid": 0.0, "semantic_action_valid": 0.0, "action_recovered": 0.0, "near_generation_limit": 1.0, "response_truncated": 1.0}]}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one