rd:\n  0 1 2\n0 . . .\n1 . X .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nO(0,0), O(0,1), O(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Winnable 2x2 move is the edge to connect and force O at center of 3x3, but this game has no center 3x3 symmetry. In 1X3 pattern, X is already at (1,1), symmetry suggests O to (1,2), (0,1) or (0,2). X has no symmetry, and the 3x3 board has empty spots; edge at symmetry of 1,1: the 3rd row (2,1) is most open to fill symmetry of (2,2) to form line of (1,2) to (2,2) to (0,2) if O takes (0,1) to (0,2) and (1,1) is already taken. But symmetry of O(0,0) or O(1,0) is open and leads to edge control, not immediate threat to force win in symmetry. O is at edge; only edge move that forms 3x3 alignment is O(2,0). No win path possible, only to block X. O can force symmetry only at 2,1, to prevent X from forming line in middle of 3x3. (1,0), (1,2) not safe, (2,0), (0,0) possible, but best move that blocks or wins: X already in 1,1. Most symmetry: 1,0 leads to 0,0 to 2,0 if O is at 2,0. But only edge is open, so best to open 2,1, so O(2,1) fills symmetry, O(2,1) is central in lower left to stop X to extend in middle. O fills O(2,0), (0,0) not possible because (0,0) not connected to center. Final: O can only stop X to go further. In no move is immediate win possible. But X is only at (1,1), so O can place at 0,1, 0,2, 1,0, 1,2, 2,0, 2,1, 2,2. To maximize symmetry and reduce chance of X win — if X wants to go to row 1,1, then to row 2,1 to win row 1 to 2. So if O occupies at (2,1), it prevents row 1,1 to go down. So move O(2,1) to block diagonal and row 2 from being full. Prevent X from completing 2x2. O blocks (2,1) — so row 2 cannot be completed in symmetry — this is better than others in edge blocking. Prevent row or column from being filled with X — O(2,1) is the edge move most likely", "episode_score": -1.1, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Winnable 2x2 move is the edge to connect and force O at center of 3x3, but this game has no center 3x3 symmetry. In 1X3 pattern, X is already at (1,1), symmetry suggests O to (1,2), (0,1) or (0,2). X has no symmetry, and the 3x3 board has empty spots; edge at symmetry of 1,1: the 3rd row (2,1) is most open to fill symmetry of (2,2) to form line of (1,2) to (2,2) to (0,2) if O takes (0,1) to (0,2) and (1,1) is already taken. But symmetry of O(0,0) or O(1,0) is open and leads to edge control, not immediate threat to force win in symmetry. O is at edge; only edge move that forms 3x3 alignment is O(2,0). No win path possible, only to block X. O can force symmetry only at 2,1, to prevent X from forming line in middle of 3x3. (1,0), (1,2) not safe, (2,0), (0,0) possible, but best move that blocks or wins: X already in 1,1. Most symmetry: 1,0 leads to 0,0 to 2,0 if O is at 2,0. But only edge is open, so best to open 2,1, so O(2,1) fills symmetry, O(2,1) is central in lower left to stop X to extend in middle. O fills O(2,0), (0,0) not possible because (0,0) not connected to center. Final: O can only stop X to go further. In no move is immediate win possible. But X is only at (1,1), so O can place at 0,1, 0,2, 1,0, 1,2, 2,0, 2,1, 2,2. To maximize symmetry and reduce chance of X win — if X wants to go to row 1,1, then to row 2,1 to win row 1 to 2. So if O occupies at (2,1), it prevents row 1,1 to go down. So move O(2,1) to block diagonal and row 2 from being full. Prevent X from completing 2x2. O blocks (2,1) — so row 2 cannot be completed in symmetry — this is better than others in edge blocking. Prevent row or column from being filled with X — O(2,1) is the edge move most likely"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player O.\n\nBoard:\n  0 1 2\n0 . . .\n1 . X .\n2 . . .\n\nLegal moves:\nO(0,0), O(0,1), O(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2)assistant\n", "response": "<reason>0,1 threatens immediate win if filled, as 2,2 is now a potential win path. But, the O in 2,2 would complete a 3x3 line and thus O can now complete the game. However the game is still open. O(0,0), and O(0,1) are both valid, but no threat. The O at 0,0 can be used to avoid blocking X from 0,1, or even win by completing the line in middle or edge. Therefore, O can now play O(1,2), completing the middle vertical or horizontal. O(1,0), O(2,2) or O(2,0) are valid. But only one move that maximizes win is O(2,2) because the middle vertical path is already occupied with middle X at 1,0. This is not a winning move yet. So now, O(2,2) is a valid move. But, in the absence of a clear threat, the safest win possibility is the one blocking the X threat. X in 1,0 means O needs to complete 2,0, 2,1. 0,0 to O is a good start, but only O in 2,2 allows win in the diagonal path 0,0 to 1,1 to 2,2. X is not blocking. But 2,1 is not filled. X is not in 2,1. So the game board does not have X in 1,1. 0,0 has no value. 1,0 is already X. So O(2,1) gives 2,1, and the line 0,0 to 1,1 to 2,2 not complete. O(0,1)? Still not possible. O must move now to block a win or create threat. O in 1,0? X already in 1,0. O in 0,0, to complete 2,2 to 0,0, middle of row 0? X in 1,1 not filled. The move must maximize future outcome. O in 0,0? X at 1,0, so O in 0,0, middle of 0th row. O at 2,0, O at 2,1 or 2,2, X at 1,0. 2,0 is empty. O in 0,0 or 2,0? 0,1 not blocked yet. 1,2? Still not winning. 2,2? 2,2 to 1,1? Still open. 1,2 to 2,2? Still open. But 0,0 → 1,1 → 2,2 is the diagonal, but X is missing at 1", "episode_score": -1.1, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>0,1 threatens immediate win if filled, as 2,2 is now a potential win path. But, the O in 2,2 would complete a 3x3 line and thus O can now complete the game. However the game is still open. O(0,0), and O(0,1) are both valid, but no threat. The O at 0,0 can be used to avoid blocking X from 0,1, or even win by completing the line in middle or edge. Therefore, O can now play O(1,2), completing the middle vertical or horizontal. O(1,0), O(2,2) or O(2,0) are valid. But only one move that maximizes win is O(2,2) because the middle vertical path is already occupied with middle X at 1,0. This is not a winning move yet. So now, O(2,2) is a valid move. But, in the absence of a clear threat, the safest win possibility is the one blocking the X threat. X in 1,0 means O needs to complete 2,0, 2,1. 0,0 to O is a good start, but only O in 2,2 allows win in the diagonal path 0,0 to 1,1 to 2,2. X is not blocking. But 2,1 is not filled. X is not in 2,1. So the game board does not have X in 1,1. 0,0 has no value. 1,0 is already X. So O(2,1) gives 2,1, and the line 0,0 to 1,1 to 2,2 not complete. O(0,1)? Still not possible. O must move now to block a win or create threat. O in 1,0? X already in 1,0. O in 0,0, to complete 2,2 to 0,0, middle of row 0? X in 1,1 not filled. The move must maximize future outcome. O in 0,0? X at 1,0, so O in 0,0, middle of 0th row. O at 2,0, O at 2,1 or 2,2, X at 1,0. 2,0 is empty. O in 0,0 or 2,0? 0,1 not blocked yet. 1,2? Still not winning. 2,2? 2,2 to 1,1? Still open. 1,2 to 2,2? Still open. But 0,0 → 1,1 → 2,2 is the diagonal, but X is missing at 1"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Tic-Tac-Toe.user\nChoose exactly one legal move that maximizes your final game outcome:\nwin is better than draw, and draw is better than loss.\n\nAnalyze the strategy as concisely as possible and choose exactly one legal action.\nDo not enumerate every legal move or the complete game tree.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>one brief reason</reason>\n<answer><SYMBOL(row,column)></answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are player O.\n\nBoard:\nYour previous response reached the generation lim